From: Mark Vis <118430440+mark-vis@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 00:47:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/28] (b) v2, part b-3: documentation of \caption* for users

Review findings F4, N2 and N6 of round 1:

- The usrguide draft said that \caption* gives "no link target for a
  \label". In fact \@currentlabel and \@currentHref are unchanged, so a
  \label after \caption* refers to the last numbered object, as after
  \section* (the kernel does not change that, for consistency with
  \section*); usrguide and ltfloat.dtx now say so and advise against a
  \label there.
- usrguide mentions captcont, whose \caption* is a numbered caption
  without list entry.
- The usrguide section carries a source comment: it should only be
  published together with hyperref support (hyperref not replacing
  \caption on kernels with the caption interface), since with hyperref
  without \DocumentMetadata \caption* still gives the old output.
---
 base/doc/usrguide.tex | 16 ++++++++++++----
 base/ltfloat.dtx      |  4 +++-
 2 files changed, 15 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/base/doc/usrguide.tex b/base/doc/usrguide.tex
index b36020d67..229cf6923 100644
--- a/base/doc/usrguide.tex
+++ b/base/doc/usrguide.tex
@@ -1524,6 +1524,11 @@ respectively
 \end{itemize}
 The naming here follows the pattern established by OpenType math parameters.
 
+% DRAFT (proposal (b) v2): publish this section only together with
+% support in hyperref (hyperref should not replace \caption on kernels
+% with the caption interface, i.e., \hyper@nopatch@caption when
+% \@kernel@caption is defined). Until then \caption* gives the old output
+% with hyperref without \DocumentMetadata, the most common setup.
 \section{Captions without a number}
 
 \begin{decl}
@@ -1532,9 +1537,11 @@ The naming here follows the pattern established by OpenType math parameters.
 Where \cs{caption} can be used (inside a float, for example), \cs{caption*}
 typesets \meta{text} as a caption without a label: the counter of the float
 is not stepped, nothing is written to the list of figures, tables, etc., and
-there is no number and no link target for a \cs{label}. The optional argument
-is accepted for symmetry with \cs{caption}; the kernel does not use it, but
-packages may.
+no link target is made. As after \cs{section*}, a \cs{label} after
+\cs{caption*} refers to the last numbered object before it (for example an
+earlier caption in the same float, or the current section), so do not use
+\cs{label} there. The optional argument is accepted for symmetry with
+\cs{caption}; the kernel does not use it, but packages may.
 
 The caption has the layout of the document class. The standard classes and
 many others omit the label and its separator (a colon or a period). A class
@@ -1549,7 +1556,8 @@ without \cs{DocumentMetadata} and \pkg{beamer}) do not know
 \cs{caption*} yet: with them \verb|\caption*{Text}| gives, as before, a
 numbered caption with the text `*' and a list entry, followed by `Text'.
 With \pkg{float} the kernel definition is used (also for floats restyled
-by \pkg{float}).
+by \pkg{float}). The \pkg{captcont} package has a \cs{caption*} of its own,
+which gives a numbered caption without list entry.
 The \pkg{caption} package supports \cs{caption*} with all of them.
 
